{"causes":["The Soviet invasion of Afghanistan in 1979 led to decades of conflict that destabilized the country and marginalized women's rights.","The rise of the Taliban in the 1990s imposed severe restrictions on women's freedoms, shaping the societal context in which the novel's characters exist.","Cultural norms surrounding gender roles in Afghan society historically limited women's access to education and autonomy, influencing the characters' life trajectories."],"effects":["The novel raised global awareness about the plight of women in Afghanistan, influencing humanitarian efforts and policy discussions.","A Thousand Splendid Suns has become a pivotal educational tool in discussions about gender equality and human rights.","The book inspired adaptations and discussions in various media, contributing to a broader understanding of Afghan culture and history."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"khaled-hosseini","sourceName":"Khaled Hosseini","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"a-thousand-splendid-suns","targetName":"A Thousand Splendid Suns","context":"Khaled Hosseini authored A Thousand Splendid Suns, which addresses the issues faced by Afghan women."},{"sourceSlug":"soviet-invasion-of-afghanistan","sourceName":"Soviet Invasion of Afghanistan","verb":"OCCURS_DURING","targetSlug":"a-thousand-splendid-suns","targetName":"A Thousand Splendid Suns","context":"The events of A Thousand Splendid Suns are set against the backdrop of the Soviet invasion of Afghanistan starting in 1979."},{"sourceSlug":"taliban-rule","sourceName":"Taliban Rule","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-thousand-splendid-suns","targetName":"A Thousand Splendid Suns","context":"The oppressive conditions of Taliban rule heavily influence the narrative and character development in A Thousand Splendid Suns."},{"sourceSlug":"women-in-afghanistan","sourceName":"Women in Afghanistan","verb":"DEFINES","targetSlug":"a-thousand-splendid-suns","targetName":"A Thousand Splendid Suns","context":"The struggles and rights of women in Afghanistan are defined and explored through the experiences of Mariam and Laila in the novel."},{"sourceSlug":"the-kite-runner","sourceName":"The Kite Runner","verb":"SUCCEEDS","targetSlug":"a-thousand-splendid-suns","targetName":"A Thousand Splendid Suns","context":"A Thousand Splendid Suns is Khaled Hosseini's second novel, following the success of The Kite Runner in 2003."}],"places":[{"name":"Herat, Afghanistan","role":"Birthplace of Mariam"},{"name":"Kabul, Afghanistan","role":"Major setting"},{"name":"Kandahar, Afghanistan","role":"Cultural context"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: A Thousand Splendid Suns","type":"Reference article","slug":"a-thousand-splendid-suns"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q860633","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Thousand_Splendid_Suns"],"tags":["literary work","Novels & Prose Fiction","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"A Thousand Splendid Suns contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"A Thousand Splendid Suns is the novel by Khaled Hosseini (born 1965), published in May 2007 — his second novel after The Kite Runner (2003) — and a #1 New York Times bestseller that remained on the bestseller list for more than a year, selling over four million copies in the United States alone. The novel follows two Afghan women — Mariam, an illegitimate girl from Herat, and Laila, a girl from Kabul — whose lives intersect when both are forced into marriage with the same abusive man, Rasheed, across the backdrop of thirty years of Afghan history: from the relative modernism of the pre-Soviet period through the Soviet occupation (1979–1989), the Mujahideen civil war (1989–1996), the Taliban regime (1996–2001), and the American invasion and its aftermath. The title comes from a 17th-century poem about Kabul by Saib-e-Tabrizi: 'One could not count the moons that shimmer on her roofs / And the thousand splendid suns that hide behind her walls.'

A Thousand Splendid Suns is the most widely read literary account of Afghan women's experience under the Taliban — its portrait of Mariam and Laila's suffering under Rasheed (whose abusive household mirrors the Taliban's treatment of women at the national level) and their solidarity, love, and eventual mutual liberation is both a personal story of survival and a political indictment of the Taliban's systematic oppression of women. The novel's empathetic, detailed portrayal of Afghan civilian life across three decades of war, displacement, and occupation — the loss of family, the destruction of Kabul, the desperate daily existence of ordinary Afghans — gave Western readers a personal human perspective on a country they had heard of primarily in terms of geopolitical strategy.

Hosseini's two Afghanistan novels — The Kite Runner and A Thousand Splendid Suns — have done more to shape the Western popular understanding of Afghanistan than any other literary works, and their commercial success established a new template for the 'literary witness' novel that combines personal narrative with political documentation of humanitarian crisis.","subjects":["Contemporary Literature","Contemporary Era","Afghan Literature","Women's History","War Literature","Khaled Hosseini","21st Century","Humanitarian Fiction"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"A Thousand Splendid Suns has significantly shaped discussions on women's rights and gender equality, influencing both policy and societal attitudes in Afghanistan and internationally.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
